#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# tools/pre-commit - refuse a commit that changes the frozen bootstrap's SOURCE
# without also committing the regenerated SNAPSHOT.
#
# docs/todo.md 7.6: "Did you -freeze after touching the emitter?" is a question a
# script should ask.
#
# The rule (manual chapter 4, "Regenerate the frozen snapshot"): any change to
#
#     languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf
#     languages/lib/compile-core.js        (the snapshot INLINES it, via include())
#
# needs `./mec -freeze languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf`, which rewrites
#
#     abnf/jsbootstrap.ll
#     abnf/jsagrammar.go
#
# and a second -freeze must produce a byte-identical file. Forget it and every
# `-frozen` leg in the suite runs the OLD emitter against the NEW one - which is
# not a clean red: the matrix demands goja and -frozen agree byte for byte, so a
# stale snapshot reads as a divergence in whatever the change happened to touch,
# and people have gone hunting for a defect that was not there.
#
# WHAT THIS CHECKS, and what it deliberately does not:
#
#   - Staged source change, no staged snapshot change  -> REFUSE. This is the
#     whole point and it is decidable from the index alone.
#   - Staged source change WITH a staged snapshot change -> allowed, WITHOUT
#     re-running -freeze. Running the real thing here would cost ~10-20 s on every
#     such commit and would have to write into the working tree mid-commit, which
#     a hook must not do. `tests/gates.sh --freeze` is the check that the snapshot
#     is actually a FIXED POINT; this hook only refuses to let you forget it
#     entirely. It is the cheap 90%, not a replacement for the gate.
#
# INSTALLING IT IS YOUR DECISION, not this repo's - hooks are not version
# controlled and a hook that appears without consent is a bad surprise:
#
#     ln -s ../../tools/pre-commit .git/hooks/pre-commit
#
# and `git commit --no-verify` bypasses it for the one commit where you mean to.

set -uo pipefail
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || exit 0

SOURCES='languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf languages/lib/compile-core.js'
SNAPSHOT='abnf/jsbootstrap.ll abnf/jsagrammar.go'

staged=$(git diff --cached --name-only)
[ -z "$staged" ] && exit 0

hit=""
for f in $SOURCES; do
    printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -qxF "$f" && hit="$hit $f"
done
[ -z "$hit" ] && exit 0

snap=""
for f in $SNAPSHOT; do
    printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -qxF "$f" && snap="$snap $f"
done
[ -n "$snap" ] && exit 0

cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-commit: THE FROZEN SNAPSHOT WAS NOT REGENERATED.

  changed and staged:$hit
  NOT staged:        $SNAPSHOT

The snapshot is what -frozen runs, and it inlines both files above. Committing
one without the other leaves every -frozen leg of the suite running the OLD
emitter, which does not report itself as staleness - it reports as a goja vs
-frozen DIVERGENCE somewhere unrelated.

Fix it:

    go build -o mec . && ./mec -freeze languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf
    git add $SNAPSHOT
    tests/gates.sh --freeze      # confirms the new snapshot is a FIXED POINT

Or, if you are certain this change cannot affect the emitted bootstrap:

    git commit --no-verify

EOF
exit 1
